# ThreadSanitizer suppressions (plan/features/0001-m0-skeleton.md).
#
# Only for uninstrumented third-party binaries (system / official Qt builds are not built with
# -fsanitize=thread, so TSan cannot see their internal synchronization). Every entry needs a
# comment with the reason. Never suppress our own code (libs/, app/).
#
# Symbolization: the sanitizer test presets (CMakePresets.json, linux-asan / linux-tsan /
# macos-asan) set DEBUGINFOD_URLS to an empty value, so llvm-symbolizer uses local debug info only
# and never blocks on an unreachable debuginfod server while matching a report against this file
# (plan/features/0002-m1-jlink-rtt.md, T1). JSON presets of schema version 6 cannot hold comments.


# QThread lifetime is managed inside uninstrumented libQt6Core: QThread::wait() synchronizes with
# its own wait condition and the pthread is never joined in a way TSan can see, so every QThread
# (e.g. Qt Quick's 'QQmlThread' type loader in tst_gui) is reported as a "thread leak". Ownership
# of our own threads is enforced by RAII (std::jthread / owned QThread with stop + wait).
thread:QThread::start

# Objects handed to another QThread: QObject::moveToThread() and the posted-event queue are guarded
# by QMutex/futex inside uninstrumented libQt6Core, so TSan misses the happens-before edge and
# reports the allocation (main thread) vs. the deletion (worker thread) as a race. Seen with
# Qt Quick's type loader thread in tst_gui.
race:QObject::moveToThread

# Queued calls to a QObject of another thread (QMetaObject::invokeMethod): Qt allocates the posted
# call event on the sending thread and deletes it in QCoreApplicationPrivate::sendPostedEvents() on
# the receiving thread, and concurrent invokeMethod() calls share internal Qt data. All of it is
# protected by QMutex (futex) inside uninstrumented libQt6Core, so TSan misses the happens-before
# edges and reports allocation vs. deletion, or two invokeMethod() calls of different threads. Seen
# with DeviceSession::wakeUp() (libs/device, ADR-0031), which does nothing but this call. Only
# reports with one access inside Qt's invokeMethod() are matched; our own cross-thread data goes
# through hio::BoundedQueue (std::mutex, instrumented) and stays checked.
race:QMetaObject::invokeMethod

# Not a suppression: the tests that use QNetworkAccessManager (firmware catalog) are not run under
# TSan at all - see libs/firmware/tests/CMakeLists.txt and app/cli/tests/CMakeLists.txt (Qt's HTTP
# thread is uninstrumented; plan/features/0003, review R11).
